mediacodec: the decoder is told how large a picture's packet may be

FFmpeg configures a MediaCodec video decoder with the stream's type, its
size and its parameter sets, and says nothing of how large its packets are.
The decoder then sizes its input buffers as it likes, and
ff_mediacodec_dec_send cuts a packet larger than one buffer over several,
with nothing to say that they are parts. A decoder that takes a whole
picture from each buffer need not make a picture of the parts.

Android's own players say it every time: ExoPlayer sets "max-input-size"
from the size of the picture (MediaCodecVideoRenderer.getCodecMaxInputSize),
and a decoder that wants larger buffers keeps its own figure. This sets the
same figure for the codecs the engine sends to MediaCodec: a picture at 12
bits a pixel, compressed no less than two to one (four to one for VP9), in
whole macroblocks for H.264, and no less than 2 MB for HEVC. For a 4K
broadcast that is 6.2 MB. Where the size of the picture is not known nothing
is set, and a packet larger than the buffer is cut as before.

Why now: a television plays its provider's 4K HEVC channels in an ExoPlayer
app and shows them black, with their sound, here. This is one thing the two
do differently when they open the same decoder. Whether it is what that
television needs is not known: there is no log from it.

On the emulator the key arrives and nothing else changes: its decoders want
6 MiB whatever they are told ("client requested max input size 6220800,
which is smaller than what component recommended (6291456); overriding"),
and 4K H.264 plays in them as before.

--- a/libavcodec/mediacodecdec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/mediacodecdec.c
@@ -305,7 +305,48 @@
     return ret;
 }
 #endif
+
+/* The most bytes one picture of the stream may come in, for MediaCodec's
+ * "max-input-size". A decoder told nothing sizes its input buffers as it
+ * likes, and a packet larger than one of them is cut over several
+ * (ff_mediacodec_dec_send), which a decoder that expects a whole picture in
+ * a buffer need not take. Reckoned as Android's own players reckon it
+ * (ExoPlayer's MediaCodecVideoRenderer.getCodecMaxInputSize): a picture at
+ * 12 bits a pixel, compressed no less than two to one (four for VP9), in
+ * whole macroblocks for H.264, and no less than 2 MB for HEVC. 0 where
+ * the size of the picture is not known, or the codec is one of the others:
+ * the decoder's own choice is left as it is. */
+static int mediacodec_max_input_size(const AVCodecContext *avctx)
+{
+    int64_t pixels, size;
+    int ratio = 2;
 
+    if (avctx->width <= 0 || avctx->height <= 0)
+        return 0;
+
+    pixels = (int64_t)avctx->width * avctx->height;
+    switch (avctx->codec_id) {
+    case AV_CODEC_ID_H264:
+        pixels = (int64_t)FFALIGN(avctx->width, 16) * FFALIGN(avctx->height, 16);
+        break;
+    case AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC:
+    case AV_CODEC_ID_VP8:
+    case AV_CODEC_ID_AV1:
+        break;
+    case AV_CODEC_ID_VP9:
+        ratio = 4;
+        break;
+    default:
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    size = pixels * 3 / (2 * ratio);
+    if (avctx->codec_id == AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC)
+        size = FFMAX(size, 2 * 1024 * 1024);
+
+    return size > INT_MAX ? 0 : (int)size;
+}
+
 static av_cold int mediacodec_decode_init(AVCodecContext *avctx)
 {
     int ret;
@@ -433,8 +474,12 @@
     ff_AMediaFormat_setString(format, "mime", codec_mime);
 
     if (avctx->codec_type == AVMEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO) {
+        int max_input_size = mediacodec_max_input_size(avctx);
+
         ff_AMediaFormat_setInt32(format, "width", avctx->width);
         ff_AMediaFormat_setInt32(format, "height", avctx->height);
+        if (max_input_size > 0)
+            ff_AMediaFormat_setInt32(format, "max-input-size", max_input_size);
     } else {
         ff_AMediaFormat_setInt32(format, "channel-count", avctx->ch_layout.nb_channels);
         ff_AMediaFormat_setInt32(format, "sample-rate", avctx->sample_rate);
